                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Easy Red is an ambitious World War II first-person shooter that combines historical military engagements, tactical squad management, and large-scale combat into an accessible battlefield experience. Developed by Marco Amadei and published by Corvostudio di Amadei Marco, the game recreates significant moments of the Normandy campaign while allowing players to participate in warfare from different perspectives. Rather than relying on elaborate cinematic sequences, it emphasizes tactical freedom, authentic military equipment, and varied combat situations. Its relatively modest presentation is balanced by an impressive collection of gameplay mechanics that appeal to enthusiasts of historical shooters.\n\nThe Normandy campaign serves as the foundation of the experience, featuring battles across Omaha, Utah, Gold, Juno, and Sword beaches. These historically significant locations introduce different battlefield conditions, ranging from exposed coastal assaults to engagements across forests, fortified positions, and open countryside. The ability to participate in operations from both Allied and German perspectives provides additional variety, allowing players to experience offensive and defensive objectives. This approach encourages experimentation with different strategies while presenting military engagements from opposing tactical viewpoints.\n\nInfantry combat combines traditional first-person shooting with simulation-inspired mechanics. Players have access to numerous World War II weapons, each designed to represent the characteristics of its historical counterpart. Weapon selection, positioning, and ammunition management influence battlefield effectiveness. The inventory system allows players to manage supplies and refill magazines, introducing an additional layer of preparation during combat. Weapon attachments also provide opportunities to customize equipment according to particular situations. These features create a more deliberate shooting experience without making the mechanics excessively complicated.\n\nOne of the game's most interesting elements is its class-based system, which allows players to assume different military responsibilities. Officers can command squads, medics provide essential support, and specialized soldiers contribute additional battlefield capabilities. Squad management encourages tactical thinking, particularly when advancing through defended territory or coordinating attacks against enemy positions. The freedom to select different roles makes engagements feel more dynamic, although the effectiveness of these mechanics depends heavily on artificial intelligence behavior and the responsiveness of allied units.\n\nVehicle combat expands the experience beyond infantry engagements. Historical military vehicles provide additional firepower and tactical possibilities, while airborne operations introduce alternative methods of entering battle. The inclusion of an expansive 8-by-8-kilometer environment further supports exploration and strategic movement. Players can approach objectives through different routes instead of following strictly predetermined paths. This freedom is particularly appealing to those who prefer military sandbox experiences, although large environments may occasionally feel less engaging when encounters are spread across considerable distances.\n\nMission variety contributes significantly to replayability. Beach assaults, defensive operations, airborne engagements, and inland battles introduce different challenges that require players to adjust their equipment and tactics. A separate survival mode offers another opportunity to experience the combat systems outside conventional campaign objectives. While the game lacks the elaborate storytelling associated with major military shooters, its emphasis on battlefield participation provides an alternative form of immersion.\n\nThe integrated mission editor is another valuable addition. Players can construct custom scenarios and share their creations through Steam Workshop, potentially extending the experience beyond its original campaign. This feature encourages creativity and experimentation, particularly among players interested in recreating historical engagements or developing unusual combat challenges. Steam achievements and Steam Cloud support provide additional conveniences, although the game's principal attraction remains its flexible battlefield gameplay.\n\nVisually, Easy Red reflects its independent development background. Environments, character models, animations, and effects are functional rather than exceptionally detailed. Nevertheless, the combination of recognizable military equipment, historical locations, and large battlefields helps establish an appropriate wartime atmosphere. Its modest technical requirements also make it relatively accessible to players with older hardware. Those expecting cutting-edge graphics, highly sophisticated artificial intelligence, or exceptionally polished animations may find certain aspects underwhelming.\n\nSteam Deck compatibility is officially categorized as Playable rather than fully Verified. Performance with the default graphics configuration is considered suitable, and the game can run successfully on SteamOS. However, handheld players should expect compromises. Some functionality is not fully accessible through the default controller configuration, touchscreen interaction or a community controller layout may occasionally be useful, and text entry can require manually opening the virtual keyboard. Some interface text may also appear small, while certain prompts can display keyboard, mouse, or non-Steam Deck controller icons. It is therefore functional on the Deck, but the experience is less seamless than a fully optimized handheld release.\n\nThe game's greatest strength is the considerable freedom it offers within its historical setting. Multiple military roles, customizable equipment, vehicle operations, opposing faction perspectives, and community-created missions combine to provide substantial gameplay variety. However, its ambitious scope also introduces limitations, particularly regarding visual refinement, artificial intelligence consistency, and overall production polish. Players seeking a cinematic campaign may find the experience comparatively straightforward, while those who enjoy tactical experimentation are more likely to appreciate its design.\n\nOverall, Easy Red is an enjoyable and surprisingly ambitious World War II shooter that prioritizes battlefield flexibility over cinematic spectacle. Its combination of historical engagements, squad-based mechanics, and customizable scenarios provides a rewarding experience for players interested in military combat. Although its technical presentation and mechanical refinement leave room for improvement, the breadth of its features makes it a worthwhile option for enthusiasts of accessible historical warfare simulations.\n\nA commendable independent military shooter offering engaging tactical possibilities, substantial gameplay variety, and historical atmosphere despite its modest presentation.\n\nRating: 7/10",
